## Data and privacy

- No telemetry is collected by this repository.
- All sample data in `inputs/` uses RFC 1918 / RFC 5737 addressing and `.example`
  domains. No real customer, Cisco-internal, or production data is included.
- Artifacts written to `outputs/` are generated locally from **your** environment and
  may contain sensitive topology information. They are excluded from version control
  by [.gitignore](.gitignore). Review before sharing.

## Generative AI disclosure

### Connecting these servers to an AI platform

The MCP servers under `mcp_servers/` are designed to be driven by AI agents. When you
connect them to a third-party AI platform, your prompts and the data returned by these
tools — which may include firewall policy and addressing information — are processed
under **that platform's** terms and privacy statement, outside the control of this
project. Review those terms before pointing an external model at a production
management plane.

### How this project was written

Generative AI coding tools were used substantially in writing the code and documentation
in this repository. Everything published here is covered by the automated checks
described in the README — unit tests, linting, type checking, static analysis, dependency
auditing, and secret scanning — which run in CI on every push and pull request.

AI assistance does not remove two limits, and both are stated here rather than left for
you to discover. Neither is a defect: this repository exists to **demonstrate** how the
FMC REST API, Ansible, and Terraform approaches compare, and how MCP servers wrap each of
them for an AI agent. It is a learning kit, not a turnkey product for your environment.

- **The write paths have never been executed against a real FMC.** They are exercised
  only by offline unit tests with mocked responses. Validate them in a lab against your
  own FMC version before relying on them. This is why the READMEs describe the write
  paths as beta.
- **FMC payload schemas differ between releases.** Every endpoint that writes carries an
  inline comment telling you to confirm the path and the payload fields in your own API
  Explorer (`https://<fmc-host>/api/api-explorer`). Enter the values that your FMC
  version documents rather than assuming the ones here match.
  `python/nat/create_manual_nat.py` is the most version-sensitive script in the
  repository.
